## 3. Web Application Enumeration

Visiting the site showed a login page for "MegaShopping" with no other visible content — no registration page, no obvious other endpoints.

**What I tried first, and why:**

1. **SQL injection** (`admin' OR 1=1--` style payloads) — this is a fast, low-cost thing to rule out early on any login form, since it's one of the most common ways to bypass authentication if input isn't sanitized. It didn't work here, which told me the login form was probably parameterizing its database queries correctly (or wasn't backed by SQL for auth at all).
2. Since SQLi failed and there was no other visible attack surface, I moved to **credential brute-forcing** with Hydra:

```bash
hydra -l admin -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt 10.129.95.192 \
  http-post-form "/:username=^USER^&password=^PASS^:F=Wrong Credentials"
```

**Result:** `admin : password` — a genuinely weak, default-feeling credential pair. This worked because there was no rate-limiting or account lockout on the login form, which let Hydra try thousands of passwords without being blocked.

> **Why this matters:** this is the first "real-world" lesson in the box. Weak passwords combined with no brute-force protection is one of the most common ways real applications get breached — it's unglamorous, but it's often the actual root cause behind headline breaches.

Once logged in, the only meaningful functionality was an **order form** that submitted raw XML to the server:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<order>
  <quantity>1</quantity>
  <item>Home Appliances</item>
  <address>whatever street</address>
</order>
```

Any time an application accepts raw XML from the user and parses it server-side, **XXE is worth testing for**.

