## 7. Key Takeaways

- **Read your error messages.** The very first "failed" XXE payload was actually a success signal — it confirmed entity resolution was happening, even though the output wasn't useful yet. Distinguishing "this technique doesn't work" from "this technique works but I'm using it wrong" is a core debugging skill in this field.
- **`SYSTEM` and forward-slash URI syntax matter mechanically**, not stylistically — both mistakes silently break the exploit rather than throwing an obviously related error.
- **`php://filter` is invaluable for reading a target's own source code** through an XXE/LFI primitive, php://filter is invaluable for reading an application's PHP source code through an XXE/LFI file-read primitive, because it lets us Base64-encode the source before it is returned.
- **Privilege escalation is a discovery-then-decision process.** Enumeration (winPEAS) surfaced multiple viable paths; the actual escalation step is often just picking the most direct one available, not necessarily the most "textbook" technique.
- **Understanding the attacker chain end-to-end is more valuable than memorizing payloads.** Each step here built directly on information gathered in the previous one (comment → username → XXE path guess → source code → key location).
- **Not every payload works on the first try, and that's normal.** The `net user` account-creation attempt against the hijacked scheduled task didn't persist, for reasons I didn't fully root-cause. Rather than getting stuck debugging it, pivoting to a reverse shell was the more time-efficient move — a real engagement (and a CTF under time pressure) rewards recognizing when to switch approach, not just persistence on one idea.
- **Verify what a shell actually gives you rather than assuming.** I expected the hijacked task to return a `SYSTEM` shell; it returned `Administrator` instead. Both are complete wins here, but confirming with `whoami` rather than assuming matters — the difference between SYSTEM and Administrator is occasionally significant on other targets, even when it isn't decisive on this one.

